schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Claim-Evidence Discipline
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Keeping the assertion, the source for it, the interpretation, and the
    uncertainty as distinct, separately reviewable things, so a reviewer can
    verify a page's factual claims rather than just admiring its prose.
  source_traditions:
    - the Regen Toolkit / Regen Knowledge Commons's own claim-evidence
      discipline (named directly in the article as "the Regen docs call this
      claim-evidence discipline")
    - Wikipedia's Good Article citation and verifiability standards
    - academic citation practice (claims requiring inline, checkable sources)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Positioned in this article as the discipline that makes a commons
    trustworthy over time, operationalized via a reviewer "spot-check" —
    pulling a sample of citations and confirming each source actually supports
    the sentence it's attached to, mirroring Wikipedia's Good Article process.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - claim-evidence separation as a formal knowledge-schema pattern (this
      toolkit's own claim-evidence schema/object type)
    - claim-evidence separation as an informal editorial habit (this article's
      practical framing — "pull three citations and verify")
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      well-written-vs-reviewed — the article's central distinction: prose
      quality and factual accuracy are separate axes, and claim-evidence
      discipline is specifically how you check the second one.
    - >-
      full-citation-audit-vs-spot-check — the article recommends a sampling
      spot-check (a few citations, not every one) as the practical, sustainable
      version of full verification.
  tensions:
    - >-
      reviewer-time-cost vs thoroughness — a full citation audit of every claim
      would be prohibitively slow for small teams; the spot-check is an
      explicit trade-off the article endorses rather than treats as a
      compromise.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "it has a citation" as equivalent to "it has been verified"
    flattens the article's whole point — a citation that doesn't actually
    support its attached sentence is exactly the failure mode the spot-check
    step exists to catch.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/editorial-workflow.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-379fb55cc1a6
